2026-11-07T06:33:13.6742408Z    if you are ready to continue.
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6742654Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6743084Z 3. Create the system users [34m(uids [30001..30008])[0m and groups [34m(gid 30000)[0m
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6743826Z    that the Nix daemon uses to run builds. To create system users
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6744470Z    in a different range, exit and run this tool again with
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6744986Z    NIX_FIRST_BUILD_UID set.
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6745273Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6745550Z 4. Perform the basic installation of the Nix files daemon.
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6745884Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6746342Z 5. Configure your shell to import special Nix Profile files, so you
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6747369Z    can use Nix.
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6747748Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6748089Z 6. Start the Nix daemon.
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6748469Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6749131Z [32m[4;32mWould you like to see a more detailed list of what I will do?
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6749993Z [0mNo TTY, assuming you would say yes :)
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6750395Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6750635Z I will:
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6750943Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6751300Z  - make sure your computer doesn't already have Nix files
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6752038Z    (if it does, I will tell you how to clean them up.)
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6752804Z  - create local users (see the list above for the users I'll make)
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6753502Z  - create a local group (nixbld)
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6754085Z  - install Nix in /nix
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6754732Z  - create a configuration file in /etc/nix
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6755466Z  - set up the "default profile" by creating some Nix-related files in
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6756162Z    /root
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6761511Z  - back up /etc/bash.bashrc to /etc/bash.bashrc.backup-before-nix
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6762802Z  - update /etc/bash.bashrc to include some Nix configuration
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6773101Z  - load and start a service (at /etc/systemd/system/nix-daemon.service
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6774199Z    and /etc/systemd/system/nix-daemon.socket) for nix-daemon
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6774655Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6775194Z [32m[4;32mReady to continue?
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6776026Z [0mNo TTY, assuming you would say yes :)
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6791677Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6792763Z [34m---- let's talk about sudo -----------------------------------------------------
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6801428Z [0mThis script is going to call sudo a lot. Normally, it would show you
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6802603Z exactly what commands it is running and why. However, the script is
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6803650Z run in a headless fashion, like this:
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6804132Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6804574Z   $ curl -L https://nixos.org/nix/install | sh
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6805418Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6806003Z or maybe in a CI pipeline. Because of that, I'm going to skip the
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6806810Z verbose output in the interest of brevity.
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6807354Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6807590Z If you would like to
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6808119Z see the output, try like this:
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6808496Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6808786Z   $ curl -L -o install-nix https://nixos.org/nix/install
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6809435Z   $ sh ./install-nix
2026-11-07T06:33:13.6809727Z 
